\f1 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 <$Scr_H::2><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f2\i\b\fs36 ## Why Use It?\
\pard\tx0\tx0\tx567\tx1134\tx1701\sl312\slmult1\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 <!$Scr_H::2><!$Scr_Ps::0>
\f0 \
* Forces Clarity: Because it is a single sentence, you cannot hide vague reasoning behind paragraphs of text.\
* Highly Scannable: Engineers can read 5 different Y-Statements in under a minute to understand a system's entire history.\
* Perfect for Git: It fits beautifully into a commit message or a pull request description.\
